Q: Is it better to use "min-height" always in place of height in css?
A: <p>No. There are plenty of times when it is sensible to use <code>height</code>. (Those times aren't when there is variable height content (including any kind of text) in elements without <code>overflow</code> set to non-default, but they exist).</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>What cross browser, W3C valid css, non-javascript "min-height" method in css for browser which do not support min-height?</p>
</blockquote>

<p>The only browser that people really care about which falls into that group is IE6, and it has a bug in which it treats <code>height</code> and <code>min-height</code> for <code>overflow: visible</code> content anyway. So:</p>

<pre><code>#foo { 
  height: 10em;
  min-height: 10em;
}

html&gt;body #foo {
  height: auto;
}
